{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>A lion's whelp, etc.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory (Zohar vol. 1, 227a) said: first <b>גּוּר</b> (whelp) and afterward <b>אַרְיֵה</b> (lion), and one needs to know why it was necessary to mention the time when he was a גוּר. Further, one needs to understand his saying <b>from the prey, my son, you have gone up.</b> And our Sages of blessed memory said that concerning his saying ״טָרֹף טֹרַף״ (Genesis 37:33), he says to him ״my son, you have gone up,״ meaning, you cleared yourself. According to their words of blessed memory, the word ״בְּנִי״ (my son) may be parsed both upward and downward on this path: <b>מִטֶּרֶף בְּנִי</b> — meaning, Joseph — <b>עָלִיתָ</b> (you went up); also on this path: <b>מִטֶּרֶף</b> (from the prey) <b>שֶׁל יוֹסֵף בְּנִי</b> — meaning, you are my son, you have gone up — and the word ״בְּנִי״ is an expression of love and closeness of heart. But one needs some flavor of why Jacob would have suspected Judah, to say now of him that he had risen from the suspicion — and reason dictates that it never entered Jacob's mind that the brothers would lay a hand on Joseph to suspect one of them; yet the words of our Sages of blessed memory are words of received tradition. Further, one needs to understand his saying <b>he couched, he lay down</b> etc. <b>who shall rouse him?</b> — for besides the fact that the plain sense of the words is not intelligible, why did he speak in hidden language and deviate from the order in which he had begun to speak to him directly, as in his saying ״you, your brothers shall praise you״ etc. ״my son, you have gone up״?" "<b>Now</b> the verses may be explained according to one pure and clear premise that has illumined our eyes with things more precious than the light of our Torah, and it is that the first man is the tree in which were suspended all the souls of holiness that have gone forth into the world from the day the world came into being and that are yet to go forth. And when the first man sinned, the evil portion gained dominion and took from him captives beyond number; and they investigated, from the book of men of truth, how great in magnitude were the captives of holiness held within the klippah. And they, the people of HaShem, strive from the day they come into being to clarify and to draw out that which was swallowed from its mouth, and they go and clarify and draw forth from its hand by means of the source of holiness that HaShem planted within us — namely, the Torah and the commandments. Also, at times, through the clinging of the holy soul to the impure soul against its will, it draws forth a living soul after its kind, and this is the sod of (Genesis 33:3) ״וַתִּדְבַּק נַפְשׁוֹ בְּדִינָה בַּת יַעֲקֹב״ — that the soul of Dinah drew holiness for a holy soul from that which was within Shechem, and they said of blessed memory (Kohelet Yaakov) that it is the soul of Rabbi Ḥanina ben Teradyon. And this is a far-reaching allusion." "And at times the soul goes forth of itself into the world because of the swarming of klippah-souls in the world — she too goes forth, and in her going forth she is distinguished from the storehouse of the klippah by her nature, and she yearns to walk in the good path. And this is the sod of the soul of converts who convert of their own accord and long to join beneath the canopy of holiness. And included in this matter as well: by means of a most reprehensible act, the Supernal One — who is a man of worthlessness — chooses to bring forth the holy soul that is captive in his hand into a place of klippah that is an aspect of sin, so that it will be even more reprehensible. And this is your sign: Ruth the Moabite emerged from the incest of father and daughter, and Naamah the Ammonite. And you can discern to know that Ruth, who emerged from the most brazen transgression — for Moab was the most brazen in sin, and her deeds testify to it, as they said of blessed memory (Bava Kamma 38b) — produced the royal chain of the House of David; while Naamah emerged from Ammon, whose sin was not as defiled as her sister's. And you should know that if the act of Lot had been performed by another man, the two good offshoots would not have emerged through him; but it was on account of Lot, who was great in his aspect — for even the aspect of evil has great gradations, one above the other according to its level. Go forth and learn from the nations, who are branches of the klippah, that among them are great kings and kings of lesser rank, and princes, etc. And this is the sod of their saying of blessed memory (Sotah 49b) ״בְּעִקְּבוֹת מְשִׁיחָא״ (in the footsteps of the Messiah) — their intent in this is that because we are clarifying every grip of the klippah from the holiness, when the clarification reaches the end of the matter, which is the heel (עָקֵב), and they called the ultimate end עֲקֵבַיִם (heels) because it is the last of the limbs of man. And therefore you will find that in former times great souls were clarified from the klippah — go forth and learn: the great man among giants, our father and the crown of our head, Abraham, the first righteous convert among converts, and Sarah our mother, and Ruth the Moabite; and in later generations, Shemayah and Avtalyon, Onkelos the convert. But today, even though they convert — because the sparks of holiness have been exhausted and spent from within the klippah and nothing remains but in the heels, the power of clarification itself is also diminished in its aspect — and this is the sod of their saying of blessed memory in the Zohar (vol. 1, 258a): ״נָגְעוּ רַגְלִין בְּרַגְלִין״ (feet touched feet), for each aspect clarifies its own aspect." "And according to this premise you will understand the counsel of HaShem that He counseled concerning Judah, and He sent an angel and compelled him to come to the harlot (Genesis Rabbah ch. 85) — for through this, even though the matter was unfit according to the Torah to lie with his daughter-in-law, and Judah was compelled by the [Divine] utterance, a matter of prohibition was hidden from him and he did not know that she was his daughter-in-law. And through this he drew forth from the source of impurity a wondrous branch of holiness — Peretz and Zerach, who had been captive in the aspect of evil, and a man of worthlessness was lying upon them because of the greatness of their merit. Go forth and learn how many righteous ones emerged from them, and the chain of the kingship of the House of David. And without Judah, who is the aspect of kingship, no man would have been able to draw them forth from his hand. Moreover, without the semblance of an evil act — that he came to his daughter-in-law — there would be no possibility of drawing them forth; and through the semblance of evil, behold, he entered the dwelling of evil and drew forth abundant spoil." "<b>And behold,</b> these are the matters that the verse stated: <b>a lion's whelp is Judah</b> — meaning, at first, before the act of Tamar, he was a גוּר (whelp), and this is what he alluded to at the beginning of his words in saying ״your brothers shall praise you,״ besides what I explained in its place. On this basis <b>your brothers shall praise you</b> may further be explained: meaning, you had only your own greatness, that your brothers would be subjugated to you — but this quality did not endure for the generations. Go forth and learn how great was the greatness of his sons alone — Peretz and Zerach, who came from the act of the web that Elohim spread — and at the end, <b>a lion</b> (אַרְיֵה), who overpowered and was exalted. And he explained from whence he merited to ascend to this lofty degree, and he said: <b>from the prey, my son, you have gone up</b> — meaning, from that prey of man — for the aspect of evil preyed upon the wondrous souls from the first man — through the act of Tamar you brought them relief; from this was your ascent, and you acquired sons whose kingship is an eternal kingship; for without this, there was no greatness for generations in the branches of your soul, but for you alone." "And the verse's saying <b>he crouched, he lay down</b> etc. — meaning: that prey through which his elevation came about — he crouched, he lay down upon him, namely that which is said of it (Genesis 4:7) ״לַפֶּתַח חַטָּאת רוֹבֵץ״, and he had no power to raise it off himself and to extract from it the precious stone." "And he hinted in his saying <b>a lion, a lioness</b>, that the forceful aspect within the soul — that is indeed the aspect of a lion — and therefore the aspect of the lion of holiness, which is Judah, is needed to raise it and extract it from under its hand. And from now on the scepter shall not depart from Judah forever; his kingship is the kingship of all worlds, for he acquired the foundation of monarchy until שִׁילֹה comes, for whom we hope day by day — we have hoped for salvation."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:51:10.450681626Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}